From 7e93fe7b86f0e449454db03d9d8eb02b55d6dcfd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JuggyMcNutty <11588877+JuggyMcNutty@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:49:54 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] VM: the commonest statements run in place

FORK ONLY. This project ships a NO-AI Code Rule asking that LLM-written
changes stay in a fork and are never PR'd upstream. These were written with
Claude. Do not submit them.

Every statement went through ExpressionEvaluator::Eval: a whole
ExpressionEvalResult (a value, a label, an iterator, the control-flow
fields) constructed and destructed, the breakpoint list walked, the
statement visited, and for a jump a binary search for its target. On
Liberty Island conditions are 45% of the statements, assignments 22%,
calls 13%, return; 8%, a foreach's next pass 6% and jumps 3%.

Frame::Run now runs those in place. A statement is classified when first
run (Expression::StatementKind), and a jump keeps its target's statement
index in its node. A condition, an assignment to a variable kind (the
typed evaluators' Assign) and a call whose value is dropped are evaluated
by an ExpressionEvaluator with no result of its own; return;, a jump and a
foreach's next pass need no evaluation. Every other statement, and every
statement while a breakpoint is set, takes Eval as before.

What the frame does is the same:

- Nothing these statements evaluate reports control flow to the frame;
  only JumpIfNot's own jump, which Run makes.
- A GotoState during a condition moves the frame to other code, and the
  jump then looks its target up in that code, as before.
- Only an object's state frame can lose its Func or wait on a latent
  function, and what its Run returns is not used: those exits return an
  empty result.

Checked with the frame time and random seeds temporarily fixed: a hash of
every actor's location, rotation, velocity, state and class matched the
build before patch 0028, frame by frame, on Liberty Island for 4,300 frames
and on UNATCO HQ for 30,000 -- every frame that build itself repeats
identically -- and so did the logs.
